 "script": "Trees are trading under your feet. Number three. Fungal threads tangle with tree roots. About 85 percent of plant species form these partnerships. The tree pays in sugar. The fungus pays back in water and nutrients. Number two. In 1997, Suzanne Simard tracked labeled carbon moving between birch and fir trees through fungi. Number one. In a Swiss forest, up to 40 percent of a tree's fine-root carbon came from its neighbors. Scientists still debate how much this actually helps. But the sugar moves. That mushroom near a park tree may be part of the network. Step lightly. Someone down there is negotiating.",
